{
  "id": "gaussian-naive-bayes",
  "title": "Gaussian Naive Bayes",
  "description": "Gaussian Naive Bayes is a classification algorithm based on Bayes' theorem with a \"naive\" assumption that features are conditionally independent given the class. Each feature's likelihood is modeled as a Gaussian distribution, making it fast and effective for many real-world problems.\n\nGiven labeled training data and unlabeled test data, predict the class for each test sample by computing the posterior probability for each class.\n\n### Algorithm\n\n1. For each class c, compute the prior probability:\n\n$$\nP(c) = \\frac{n_c}{n}\n$$\n\n2. For each class c and feature j, compute the mean and population variance:\n\n$$\n\\mu_{cj} = \\frac{1}{n_c} \\sum_{i \\in c} x_{ij}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2_{cj} = \\frac{1}{n_c} \\sum_{i \\in c} (x_{ij} - \\mu_{cj})^2\n$$\n\n3. For each test sample, compute the log posterior for each class:\n\n$$\n\\log P(c | x) \\propto \\log P(c) + \\sum_j \\left[ -\\frac{1}{2} \\log(2\\pi\\sigma^2_{cj}) - \\frac{(x_j - \\mu_{cj})^2}{2\\sigma^2_{cj}} \\right]\n$$\n\n4. Predict the class with the highest log posterior. Add a small epsilon (1e-9) to variances to avoid division by zero.\n\nReturn one integer class label for each row of X_test.",
  "seoDescription": "Fit Gaussian Naive Bayes class statistics and predict labels from priors and feature likelihoods.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X_train = [[1], [2], [3], [10], [11], [12]], y_train = [0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1], X_test = [[2], [11], [6]]\n\nOutput: [0, 1, 0]\n\nExplanation: Each test value is assigned to the class with the larger Gaussian log posterior.",
    "Input: X_train = [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1], [10, 10], [11, 10], [10, 11]], y_train = [0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1], X_test = [[0.5, 0.5], [10.5, 10.5]]\n\nOutput: [0, 1]"
  ],
  "requirements": "* Compute the prior P(c) = n_c / n for each class\n* Compute the mean and population variance (divide by n_c, not n_c - 1) for each feature per class\n* Add epsilon = 1e-9 to all variances to handle zero-variance features\n* Compute log posteriors and predict the class with the highest value\n* Return a list of predicted class labels for the test set",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Gaussian Naive Bayes\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world data is inherently stochastic and incomplete. Probability theory and statistics provide principled methods to quantify uncertainty, model data-generating distributions, and evaluate confidence in empirical predictions. **Gaussian Naive Bayes** computes statistical moments, likelihoods, or uncertainty metrics across observation samples.\n\nIn this problem, the objective is to implement **Gaussian Naive Bayes** efficiently and accurately adhering to standard mathematical conventions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe operation is governed by the following mathematical formulation:\n\n$$\nP(c) = \\frac{n_c}{n}\n$$\n\nEvery variable and index in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1: Input Validation & Pre-processing**: Validate tensor dimensions, convert inputs into contiguous array buffers, and extract structural shape parameters.\n2. **Phase 2: Core Transformation**: Apply vectorized operations directly across array dimensions without explicit Python loops.\n3. **Phase 3: Aggregation & Normalization**: Compute required summations, projections, or element-wise bounds while safeguarding against numerical instability.\n4. **Phase 4: Output Formatting**: Ensure the resulting array or dictionary matches expected output data types and precision tolerances.\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Log-Space Calculations**: Probabilities can be extremely small; computing log-likelihoods prevents floating-point underflow when multiplying many probability densities.\n* **Maximum Likelihood Estimation**: Many machine learning loss functions (like Cross-Entropy and Mean Squared Error) directly derive from minimizing negative log-likelihood under specific distributional assumptions.\n* **Outlier Sensitivity**: High-order moments (skewness and kurtosis) reveal distribution heavy-tails, signaling when robust statistics or clipping are necessary.",
