  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nVectorized, numerically stable reference implementation following exact specifications.\n\n### 💻 Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport math\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef gaussian_naive_bayes(X_train: list, y_train: list, X_test: list) -> list:\n    Xt = np.array(X_train, dtype=float)\n    yt = np.array(y_train)\n    Xte = np.array(X_test, dtype=float)\n    classes = np.unique(yt)\n    stats = {}\n    for c in classes:\n        Xc = Xt[yt == c]\n        stats[c] = (len(Xc) / len(Xt), Xc.mean(axis=0), Xc.var(axis=0) + 1e-9)\n    preds = []\n    for x in Xte:\n        best_c, best_logp = None, -1e18\n        for c, (prior, mean, var) in stats.items():\n            logp = math.log(prior) - 0.5 * np.sum(np.log(2 * np.pi * var) + ((x - mean)**2) / var)\n            if logp > best_logp:\n                best_logp = logp\n                best_c = c\n        preds.append(int(best_c))\n    return preds\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the input dimensions.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ for memory buffers.\n",
